PATCH 193: promote + reconstruct the 22dd op-0xc display-list geometry-record poster (0x41d0 -> db99 ->
ddff), advancing the mission-viewport interpreter one more handler (trap 0x041d0 -> 0x03fba, observed).

The in-mission 22dd threaded-code interpreter (FUN_0000_22dd) dispatches handlers through the cursor
`fist_icall_near(0, word[DGROUP:word[0x4a86]])`.  After patch 192 the FIRST handler 0x23ea (trig) runs and
the cursor advances to 0x41d0 -- an engine MID-ENTRY Ghidra never promoted (reached only through the 22dd
handler-offset table), so fist_icall_near trapped it and the cursor never advanced.  Confirmed live:
FIST_TRACE_TRAPS=1 on the ACCEPT cascade logged `[icall] TRAP unmapped indirect call -> linear 0x041d0`.

0x41d0 (`call 0xdb99; word[0x4b9e]=0; addw 2,[0x4a86]; ret`) posts an op-0xc display-list GEOMETRY record
to the Doug-Huffman extender.  The real work is the record builder 0xdb99 and its common prolog 0xddff.
asm-verified vs re_out/fist_dat_image.bin (DS=SS=DGROUP=0x1c00):

  ddff: es:di = word[0xea2e]:word[0xea2c] (extender TCB record base = the patch-068 DAT_2000_aa2c macro);
        si=word[0x156a] (dst geom DGROUP NEAR-OFFSET) -> record[0x0e/0x12/0x22/0x24/0x10/0x14/0x1e/0x20/0x2]
        (width/height halves + origin); si=word[0x1552] (src geom=0x7aa4) -> record[0x16..0x1c].
        Ghidra typed DAT_1000_d56a as int** (host ptr) so `DAT_1000_d56a[3]` wild-dereffed the near-offset
        -- base-loss (the patch-186 class); rebased onto g_mem+0x1c000+near_offset with uint16 fields.
  db99: call ddff; si=0x1548 (immediate) -> copy d548 struct [0x12..0x1c] -> record[0x2c..0x36];
        record[0x38]=word[0x7b1c]; record[0x3e]=word[0x7b18]>>2; record[2]=word[word[0x156a]]; record[4]=0;
        inbox(record+0x3f2)=EBX; op(word[0xea10])=0xc; call e339 (-> 0x8799 gate -> fist_extender_gate).

EBX ("inbox", record+0x3f2) IS PROVABLY DEAD FOR OP 0xc.  The extender PM gate FUN_00000f30
(re_out/fist_image.bin) does `ebx=[current-TCB+0x3f2]; call handler`, but the op-0xc handler
[0xcb3+0xc]=0x78f0's FIRST instruction is `mov ebx,[0xc93]` -- it overwrites ebx with the current-TCB
before any use.  0x78f0 then reads the RECORD fields (+0x2/+0x4 render-target seg:off, +0x1e width,
+0x22 height -- exactly the fields db99/ddff build) and renders the viewport.  So the value posted in EBX
is never observed for op 0xc; db99's `mov %ebx,%gs:0x3f2(%si)` writes the ambient carried hand-asm
register (db99 never sets EBX) which the flat-C model drops -- since it is provably unread we write 0 and
DO NOT fabricate a value.  The op-0xc CONSUMER (0x78f0, Route-1 extender work) is located but not wired
here: fist_extender_gate logs op 0xc and returns 0, and 0x41d0 advances the cursor regardless (crash-free)
-- wiring the extender renderer awaits a verifiable frame (the geometry it reads is still the uninitialised
0x7aa4/0x156a viewport rect; the render is not bit-verifiable until the voxel raycaster + AE).

OBSERVATION GATE (per doctrine -- render not yet verifiable): with FIST_TRACE_TRAPS=1 on the cascade the
trap moves FORWARD from linear 0x041d0 to linear 0x03fba (the NEXT 22dd handler), `[ext] service op 0x0c
... inbox=00000000` fires once (db99 built the record + posted op 0xc), and there is no SEGV.  Structurally
asm-faithful.

Behaviour-neutral for the 19 verify flows: the handler is reached only through the d548-gated 22dd loop
(d548==0 in the menu), and 0x41d0/db99 have no direct callers -- the ddff base-loss fix only changes a
function that was WRONG (wild host-ptr deref) and is reached only on the mission path.  verify.sh both
stays 19/19; default boot rc=0; mainmenu md5 3a6ff1c5 unchanged.  Sources stay pristine (re_out/fist.c
61453e42); fist.c via build/.

